@node	3.1.1, 3.1.2, 3.1, 3.1
@subsection Local State Variables

To illustrate what we mean by having a computational object with time-varying
state, let us model the situation of withdrawing money from a bank account.  We
will do this using a function @code{withdraw}, which takes as argument an
@code{amount} to be withdrawn.  If there is enough money in the account to
accommodate the withdrawal, then @code{withdraw} should return the balance
remaining after the withdrawal.  Otherwise, @code{withdraw} should raise the
typed error @code{WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds}, the one book string this
edition types this way (see the conventions of the companion).  For example, if
we begin with $100 in the account, we should obtain the following sequence of
responses using @code{withdraw}:

@example kotlin
sealed interface WithdrawError @{
    data object InsufficientFunds : WithdrawError
@}

var balance: Long = 100L

fun withdraw(amount: Long): Either<WithdrawError,
    Long> =
    if (amount > balance) @{
        Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
    @} else @{
        balance -= amount
        Either.Right(balance)
    @}

withdraw(25L)
// => Either.Right(75)

withdraw(25L)
// => Either.Right(50)

withdraw(60L)
// => Either.Left(InsufficientFunds)

withdraw(15L)
// => Either.Right(35)
@end example

@noindent
Observe that the expression @code{withdraw(25L)}, evaluated twice, yields
different values.  This is a new kind of behavior for a function.  Until now,
all our functions could be viewed as specifications for computing mathematical
functions.  A call to a function computed the value of the function applied to
the given arguments, and two calls to the same function with the same
arguments always produced the same result.@footnote{Actually, this is not quite
true.  One exception was the random-number generator in @ref{1.2.6}.
Another exception involved the operation/type tables we introduced in
@ref{2.4.3}, where the values of two calls to @code{get} with the same
arguments depended on intervening calls to @code{put}.  On the other hand,
until we introduce assignment, we have no way to create such functions
ourselves.}

To implement @code{withdraw}, we can use a variable @code{balance} to indicate
the balance of money in the account and define @code{withdraw} as a function
that accesses @code{balance}.  The @code{withdraw} function checks to see if
@code{balance} is at least as large as the requested @code{amount}.  If so,
@code{withdraw} decrements @code{balance} by @code{amount} and returns the new
value of @code{balance}.  Otherwise, @code{withdraw} raises the
@emph{Insufficient funds} error, as shown above: @code{balance} is an ordinary
top-level @code{var}, freely visible and modifiable by any other declaration in
the program.

@noindent
Decrementing @code{balance} is accomplished by the statement

@example kotlin
balance -= amount
@end example

@noindent
This is Kotlin's ordinary compound-assignment operator, working directly on
the @code{var} named @code{balance}.  @code{Balance -= amount} changes
@code{balance} so that its new value is the result obtained by evaluating
@code{balance - amount}: the previous value of @code{balance} minus
@code{amount}.@footnote{Kotlin needs no special naming convention for
mutation the way the original book's assignment form does: assigning to a @code{var} is
visibly different from an ordinary call, and the compiler itself refuses the
assignment unless the name was declared @code{var}.  Data structures that
mutate their own fields, as we will see in @ref{3.3}, follow the same
rule directly on their properties.}

@code{Withdraw}'s @code{if} branch does two things in sequence when the
balance suffices: first decrementing @code{balance} and then returning the new
value of @code{balance}.  In general, a Kotlin block already sequences its
statements this way: @code{@{ balance -= amount; balance @}} runs the first
statement for its effect and returns the second as the block's
value.@footnote{The original book needs a separate sequencing form for
side-effecting expressions, because the body of one of its procedures and
each clause of its conditionals are themselves single expressions.  Kotlin's function
bodies and each branch of a @code{when} are blocks of statements already, so
no equivalent form is needed anywhere in this book.}

Although @code{withdraw} works as desired, the variable @code{balance} presents
a problem.  As specified above, @code{balance} is a name defined at the top
level and is freely accessible to be examined or modified by any other
declaration.  It would be much better if we could somehow make @code{balance}
internal to @code{withdraw}, so that @code{withdraw} would be the only
function that could access @code{balance} directly and any other function
could access @code{balance} only indirectly (through calls to @code{withdraw}).
This would more accurately model the notion that @code{balance} is a local
state variable used by @code{withdraw} to keep track of the state of the
account.

We can make @code{balance} internal to @code{withdraw} by rewriting the
definition as follows:

@example kotlin
fun newWithdraw(): (Long) -> Either<WithdrawError,
    Long> @{
    var b = 100L
    return @{ amount ->
        if (amount > b) @{
            Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
        @} else @{
            b -= amount
            Either.Right(b)
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
What we have done here is declare a local @code{var b}, bound to the initial
value 100.  Within this local environment, we build a lambda that takes
@code{amount} as an argument and behaves like our previous @code{withdraw}
function.  This function, returned as the result of calling
@code{newWithdraw()}, behaves in precisely the same way as @code{withdraw}
but whose variable @code{b} is not accessible by any other
function.@footnote{In programming-language jargon, the variable @code{b}
is said to be @newterm{encapsulated} within the closure @code{newWithdraw}
returns.  Encapsulation reflects the general system-design principle known
as the @newterm{hiding principle}: One can make a system more modular and
robust by protecting parts of the system from each other; that is, by
providing information access only to those parts of the system that have a
``need to know.''}

Combining a captured @code{var} with local variables is the general
programming technique we will use for constructing computational objects with
local state.  Unfortunately, using this technique raises a serious problem:
when we first introduced functions, we also introduced the informal model of
substituting argument values into function bodies
(@ref{1.1.5}) to provide an interpretation of what a function
call means.  We said that calling a function should be interpreted as
evaluating the body of the function with the parameters replaced by
their values.  The trouble is that, as soon as we introduce a captured @code{var} into our
language, substitution is no longer an adequate model of a function call.
(We will see why this is so in @ref{3.1.3}.)  As a consequence, we
technically have at this point no way to understand why the @code{newWithdraw}
function behaves as claimed above.  In order to really understand a function
such as @code{newWithdraw}, we will need to develop a new model of function
application.  In @ref{3.2} we will introduce such a model, together
with an explanation of the captured @code{var} and local variables.  First, however, we
examine some variations on the theme established by @code{newWithdraw}.

The following function, @code{makeWithdraw}, creates ``withdrawal
processors.''  The parameter @code{balance} in @code{makeWithdraw}
specifies the initial amount of money in the account.@footnote{In contrast with
@code{newWithdraw} above, we do not have to declare a separate local @code{var}
to make @code{balance} local, since a function's parameters are already
local.  This will be clearer after the discussion of the environment model of
evaluation in @ref{3.2}.  (See also @ref{Exercise 3.10}.)}

@example kotlin
fun makeWithdraw(balance: Long): (Long) -> Either<WithdrawError,
    Long> @{
    var b = balance
    return @{ amount ->
        if (amount > b) @{
            Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
        @} else @{
            b -= amount
            Either.Right(b)
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{MakeWithdraw} can be used as follows to create two objects @code{w1} and
@code{w2}:

@example kotlin
val w1 = makeWithdraw(100L)
val w2 = makeWithdraw(100L)

w1(50L)
// => Either.Right(50)

w2(70L)
// => Either.Right(30)

w2(40L)
// => Either.Left(InsufficientFunds)

w1(40L)
// => Either.Right(10)
@end example

@noindent
Observe that @code{w1} and @code{w2} are completely independent objects, each
with its own local state variable @code{b}.  Withdrawals from one do not
affect the other.

We can also create objects that handle deposits as well as withdrawals, and
thus we can represent simple bank accounts.  Here is a function that returns a
``bank-account object'' with a specified initial balance.  The original book's
dispatch procedure, which hands back the withdraw or
deposit closure for a message symbol, becomes this edition's
@code{Account} interface: the dispatch the book's conditional approximates is
an ordinary method call in Kotlin, and @code{makeAccount} returns an object
implementing that interface directly, rather than a bare closure, because the
book has now given the object a real protocol of named operations.

@example kotlin
interface Account @{
    fun withdraw(amount: Long): Either<WithdrawError,
        Long>

    fun deposit(amount: Long): Long
@}

fun makeAccount(balance: Long): Account @{
    var b = balance
    return object : Account @{
        override fun withdraw(
            amount: Long,
        ): Either<WithdrawError, Long> =
            if (amount > b) @{
                Either.Left(WithdrawError.InsufficientFunds)
            @} else @{
                b -= amount
                Either.Right(b)
            @}

        override fun deposit(amount: Long): Long @{
            b += amount
            return b
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Each call to @code{makeAccount} sets up an environment with a local state
variable @code{b}.  Within this environment, @code{makeAccount} builds an
anonymous object implementing @code{withdraw} and @code{deposit}, both
closing over the one captured @code{b}.  The returned object itself
represents the bank-account object; calling its methods is precisely the
@newterm{message-passing} style of programming that we saw in
@ref{2.4.3}, although here we are using it in conjunction with the
ability to modify local variables.

@code{MakeAccount} can be used as follows:

@example kotlin
val acc = makeAccount(100L)

acc.withdraw(50L)
// => Either.Right(50)

acc.withdraw(60L)
// => Either.Left(InsufficientFunds)

acc.deposit(40L)
// => 90

acc.withdraw(60L)
// => Either.Right(30)
@end example

@noindent
Each call on @code{acc} invokes the locally defined @code{deposit} or
@code{withdraw} method, which is then applied to the specified
@code{amount}.  As was the case with @code{makeWithdraw}, another call to
@code{makeAccount}

@example kotlin
val acc2 = makeAccount(100L)
@end example

@noindent
will produce a completely separate account object, which maintains its own
local @code{balance}.

